t with a real-world distribution while preserving diversity (specifically, no over-representation of ultra-long carbon chains).\n\nThe design makes feedback *the only experimentally varied factor*: all arms share one pretrained backbone, identical initialization, identical sampling temperature, identical per-step budgets, and identical score computation. Any between-arm difference in property scores, validity/hallucination rates, or diversity/distribution-alignment metrics is then attributable to the reward composition. A reward-decomposition scheme (Arm B: properties only; Arm C: properties + affinity) further isolates the marginal contribution of binding affinity, and a shuffled-reward negative control (Arm D) plus a reward-injection sanity check verify that the RL mechanism behaves as expected rather than improving scores spuriously.\n\nBecause REINFORCE without regularization collapses to degenerate outputs that look \"high-scoring\" but lose diversity, every RL arm uses a KL-to-prior penalty and per-batch reward normalization (standard augmented-likelihood objective). This is a deliberate confound-control decision: it makes any residual diversity loss attributable to feedback itself rather than to unconstrained RL. Diversity is therefore measured as a primary outcome, not a by-product.\n\n**Causal chain tested:** reward containing targeted-property terms \u2192 policy concentrates mass on molecules that score well on those exact descriptors \u2192 but a KL prior anchors output to the real drug-like distribution. Whether anchoring survives (distribution alignment) and whether hallucination drops are the empirical question the arms answer.\n\n## 2. Arms / Controls\n\n**Shared backbone (all arms):** a REINVENT-style SMILES autoregressive RNN (3-layer GRU, hidden=256, embedding=128), producing canonical-SMILES tokens from a fixed SMILES vocabulary, pretrained by teacher-forcing MLE on a drug-like corpus (proposed: ZINC-250k or a ChEMBL drug-like subsample). The same saved pretrained checkpoint seeds every arm.\n\n**RL formulation (all arms except baseline):** REINFORCE/policy-gradient with augmented likelihood:\n`L = \u2212E[ (log R \u2212 \u03c3 \u00b7 KL_to_prior) ]` per sampled molecule, with per-batch reward normalization (subtract batch mean, divide by batch std), KL coefficient \u03c3 fixed a priori, and identical optimizer/learning-rate schedule across arms.\n\n**Arm A \u2014 Baseline (no feedback):** freezes the pretrained model, or continues training with MLE on the target distribution only. Samples with the same temperature and step budget as the RL arms. Provides the property/hallucination/diversity floor.\n\n**Arm B \u2014 Partial chemical feedback:** RL fine-tuning with reward = f(p-logP, QED) only (no affinity term). Isolates the effect of the two property-based terms.\n\n**Arm C \u2014 Full chemical feedback (treatment):** RL fine-tuning with reward = f(p-logP, QED, binding-affinity surrogate). The primary comparison arm.\n\n**Arm D \u2014 Negative control (optional):** RL fine-tuning where sampled molecules' rewards are shuffled/shredded (rewards decoupled from molecules). Expected to show no stable reward increase; guards against reward-injection artifacts, seed luck, or optimization noise masquerading as improvement.\n\n**Sanity check (mandatory, Arm C):** plot/inspect the composite reward as a function of RL step; require a monotone-increasing trend (reward injection demonstrably works) and a final reward significantly above the Arm-A sampled reward under the identical scoring backend.\n\n**Control bookkeeping (all RL arms):** identical pretrained checkpoint, identical seed list (\u22655 seeds, propose 10 if budget allows), identical number of RL updates (proposed: 5,000 updates \u00d7 batch 64), identical sampling temperature (1.0), identical \u03c3 for the KL penalty, identical docking/oracle settings. Metrics are always computed per-seed on the same number of generated unique molecules (proposed: N=2,000 valid unique molecules per seed).\n\n## 3. Feedback formulation\n\n**Composite reward (Arm C):**\n`R(s) = w_p \u00b7 z_p(p-logP(s)) + w_q \u00b7 z_q(QED(s)) + w_b \u00b7 z_b(affinity(s))`\n- p-logP(s): penalized logP computed by RDKit (lipophilicity penalized for excessive ring count and heavy-atom size \u2014 structurally the term that discourages ultra-long-chain over-representation).\n- QED(s): quantitative estimate of drug-likeness (RDKit).\n- affinity(s): predicted binding affinity to the chosen target (see oracle below).\n- z_\u00b7 : per-entity normalization to [0,1] using fixed a priori anchor ranges (e.g., p-logP in [\u22122, +6], QED in [0,1], affinity in [\u221212, 0] kcal/mol from a held-out calibration subset), fixed before training and reported. Arm B drops the w_b term; Arm A has no reward.\n- Weights w_p=w_q=1/3, w_b=1/3 by default, fixed a priori; a small planned grid (two alternate weightings) may be run as a robustness check but is not required for the primary claim.\n\n**Binding-affinity oracle (primary proposal):** SMINA/AutoDock-Vina rigid-receptor docking against one fixed, pre-chosen druggable target pocket (proposed: EGFR kinase domain, e.g., PDB 1M17, pocket defined by the co-crystal ligand), fixed exhaustiveness and random seed so every arm sees an identical score backend. Receptor/Ligand preparation fixed (protonation, grid box). Fallback if docking is computationally infeasible: a pretrained SMILES\u2192affinity predictor on the same target (e.g., a graph-convolutional affinity model) used identically across all arms. The oracle is chosen once and never swapped mid-experiment.\n\n**Target-selection choice:** one clinically relevant druggable target with known co-crystal structures and a defined pocket; chosen so that \"targeted molecule discovery\" is concrete (generate novel potent, drug-like ligands for this pocket), and reported so the result is target-contested (any claim is scoped to that target).\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\n\n**Target/distribution reference (fixed, identical for all arms):** the drug-like training corpus itself (e.g., ZINC-250k or a ChEMBL subsample) serves as the \"real-world distribution.\" Its descriptor/scaffold statistics are computed once and held out of training of the score normalization.\n\n**Outcome metric families (each computed per seed on the 2,000 unique generated molecules):**\n\n1. **Property scores** \u2014 mean, median, and fraction-above-threshold of p-logP (e.g., fraction > 0.5 and < 5), QED (e.g., fraction > 0.6), and affinity (e.g., fraction \u2264 \u22128 kcal/mol). Reported separately and as the composite.\n\n2. **Validity / hallucination rates** \u2014 validity = fraction of sampled SMILES that (i) parse via RDKit `MolFromSmiles`, (ii) pass sanitization, and (iii) have valid valences; hallucination rate defined as 1 \u2212 validity, plus a separate \"implausibility\" flag: valid SMILES that fail a fixed physicochemical plausibility filter (e.g., unrealistic atom types, failed substructure sanity checks, impossible stereochemistry). Novelty (fraction not present in the training corpus) is reported separately as a de novo-generation property, not conflated with hallucination.\n\n3. **Diversity / distribution alignment** \u2014 internal Tanimoto diversity (mean pairwise Tanimoto over Morgan radius-2, 1024-bit fingerprints of unique molecules); number and ratio of unique Bemis\u2013Murcko scaffolds (scaffolds per unique molecule); carbon-chain-length metric: per molecule, length of the longest contiguous non-aromatic carbon chain, then report the fraction of generated molecules with chain length above a fixed threshold (proposed: >8 carbons) compared to the same fraction in the reference; carbon-count histogram; alignment to the reference via Wasserstein-1 distance on carbon count, ring count, MW, and logP descriptor distributions; reference-scaffold recovery = fraction of reference top-k scaffolds (proposed k=1,000) reproduced among generated scaffolds.\n\n4. **Statistical testing** \u2014 all comparisons are paired within-seed deltas (treatment \u2212 baseline: Arm C \u2212 A, B \u2212 A, C \u2212 B). Report mean delta \u00b1 95% CI over seeds; test the one-sided alternative that the treatment arm improves each metric using a permutation test or Wilcoxon signed-rank across seeds; apply Holm\u2013Bonferroni across the metric family. A diversity metric being *no worse* (within a pre-registered equivalence margin, e.g., carbon-chain-over-reliance ratio not exceeding reference rate + 5%) is treated as a distinct claim to be tested separately from property improvement.\n\n**Reporting:** a table of per-arm means (property, validity/hallucination, diversity/alignment), paired-delta CIs, p-values, the reward-trajectory sanity plot for Arm C, and the Arm-D control result. Any null/negative finding on alignment or hallucination is reported as a legitimate answer (the claim is falsifiable).\n\n## 5. Feasibility, provisioning, and assumptions vs. known items\n\n**Provisioning step (acknowledged as not executable offline):** the design requires `pip install rdkit numpy torch` (optionally `pandas`), and docking tooling (SMINA binary + receptor-prep via ADFRsuite, or the fallback predictor) plus the dataset snapshot. The current environment (system Python 3.14.4, no chem/ML stack; GPU disabled; **network disabled**) cannot fetch packages or datasets, so the full experiment must run in a provisioned environment. A fallback mitigation for design-time verification: the RL loop and reward bookkeeping are implementable with pure-Python utilities even without RDKit, but the complete metric suite (p-logP, QED, fingerprints, scaffolds) requires RDKit, so this fallback is a smoke test only, not a proxy measurement.\n\n**Design-time verifiable checks (these can be run once dependencies exist):** (i) unit-check p-logP/QED implementations against hand-computed values for a few small molecules (e.g., benzene, ethanol) to confirm descriptor ranges; (ii) confirm the identical molecule set produces identical scores across arms (determinism of the oracle/backend); (iii) smoke-run the RL loop for a few updates on CPU to verify the augmented-likelihood objective and per-batch normalization execute; (iv) confirm seed reproducibility by rerunning one seed and checking metric equality.\n\n### Assumptions (proposed, with alternatives) vs. known items\n\n| Item | Assumption (proposed design choice) | Alternative |\n|---|---|---|\n| Binding-affinity oracle | SMINA/AutoDock-Vina docking vs EGFR pocket (PDB 1M17) | pretrained SMILES\u2192affinity predictor; other druggable target |\n| Dataset / real-world reference | ZINC-250k or ChEMBL drug-like subsample | any fixed drug-like SMILES corpus with curated descriptors |\n| Generative backbone | REINVENT-style SMILES GRU (REINFORCE + KL-to-prior) | any autoregressive SMILES LM; same control discipline applies |\n| Targeted discovery task | novel valid molecules in a drug-like window with affinity \u2264 threshold for one chosen pocket | generic property-profile target without a protein pocket |\n| Hallucination definition | 1 \u2212 (RDKit parse+sanitize+valency validity) plus a separate plausibility-filter violation rate | stricter SMILES-token grammaticality checks |\n| Compute / step budget | 5,000 RL updates \u00d7 batch 64, temperature 1.0, \u22655 seeds (10 if budget allows), 2,000 unique molecules/seed | adjusted to available budget; must stay identical across arms |\n| Reward weights / normalization | w_p=w_q=w_b=1/3, z\u00b7 in [0,1] via fixed a priori anchors | alternate weightings as a robustness grid (optional) |\n\n**Known/fixed items from the brief:** the task is design-only; repositories and compute are not required; no datasets are pre-mounted; environment has no chem/ML stack; network is disabled. These constrain the deliverable to a specification, not an execution.\n\n**Remaining uncertainties:** the researcher-supplied status of the affinity oracle and 